Q: Spring Security: saved SecurityContext got a Null authentication A: <p>you can change priority of your filters calling.</p>
<pre><code>protected void configureOAuth(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
            .csrf()
            .disable();
    http
            .addFilterAfter(oauthFilter(), BasicAuthenticationFilter.class)
            .authorizeRequests()
            .antMatchers(&quot;/ssoep.lti.do*&quot;).authenticated();

    http.addFilterAfter(YOUR_FILTER, SecurityContextPersistenceFilter.class);
}
</code></pre>
<p>because <code>SecurityContextPersistenceFilter</code> reset context and authenticated user is clear from context.</p>

Q: How can I detect and visualize multiple touch points with JavaScript? A: <p>key everything off <code>touch.identifier</code> and keep your own map:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const active = new Map();

const put = e =\&gt; { for (const t of e.changedTouches) active.set(t.identifier, {x: t.clientX, y: t.clientY}); draw(); };

const drop = e =\&gt; { for (const t of e.changedTouches) active.delete(t.identifier); draw(); };

el.addEventListener('touchstart', put, {passive: false});

el.addEventListener('touchmove', e =\&gt; { e.preventDefault(); put(e); }, {passive: false});

el.addEventListener('touchend', drop);

el.addEventListener('touchcancel', drop);
</code></pre>
<p>use <code>changedTouches</code> in the handlers - thats only the fingers involved in that specific event, which is what you want for add/update/remove. <code>touches</code> is every finger on the screen, <code>targetTouches</code> is every finger on that element; both are fine if you'd rather just rebuild the whole set each frame from <code>e.touches</code>.</p>
<p>Also set <code>touch-action: none</code> on the element or the browser will steal moves for scrolling. if you don't need iOS-specific stuff, pointer events (<code>pointerdown</code>/<code>pointermove</code>, keyed on <code>pointerId</code>) do the same thing and cover mouse and pen too.</p>
